  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Contact Zones shows early signs of use and visibility rather than citation-driven scholarly uptake. The clearest signal is sustained downloading, with 2,077 recorded downloads, alongside a small amount of public discourse captured through Crossref. The book was also supported by KU Leuven and the Terra Foundation for American Art, and it is available in open form through repository and publisher channels.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2020, Contact Zones examines the role of photography in U.S. migrant histories and the ways photographic practice mediates cultural encounter, dislocation, and the making of new identities. The record points to a work that has reached readers and circulation channels steadily, even though formal citation uptake has not yet emerged in the indexed scholarly record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact evidence is usage: 2,077 downloads were recorded through OAPEN metrics, indicating recurring reader access over time rather than a single burst of attention. The book\u2019s open availability likely supports that reach, and repository indexing shows multiple open instances. That pattern suggests the work is being encountered in practice, even if citation-based influence remains limited in the available data.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a modest public-discourse signal, with Crossref capturing two mentions. The record does not provide enough detail to characterize the nature of those mentions, but it does indicate that the book has entered some broader public or scholarly conversation beyond its immediate publication context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Support from KU Leuven and the Terra Foundation for American Art situates the book within a funded research environment, which is consistent with its subject matter and likely helped enable publication and dissemination. Teaching or library adoption can only be stated cautiously from the record: WorldCat holdings are present, but the digest does not provide course-listing evidence.</p>",
